  "configuration_id": "config_039",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_033 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, transformer, weighted) achieves the best validation accuracy (0.713) and F1 (0.695), substantially outperforming the best RNA-only model (config_005/006 at ~0.65), confirming that the image modality contributes complementary information in fusion. However, image-only models are very weak (0.21-0.23), so it is unclear whether the image encoder quality limits the multimodal gain or whether the current resnet50 features are already sufficient. Question: Does replacing resnet50 with imagenet_vit_l in the multimodal fusion pipeline further improve performance? config_039 changes only the image encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l relative to config_033, giving a clean single-variable comparison. Why this is the best next experiment: It directly exploits the strongest configuration while resolving a key uncertainty about the image representation's contribution. If imagenet_vit_l helps, it identifies a clear path to push performance higher; if it does not, it confirms that the image signal is inherently limited and we should focus on RNA-side improvements (e.g., scgpt encoder or aggregation changes) instead. This is more informative than, e.g., testing avgpool vs. transformer aggregation (which showed little difference in RNA-only scvi_n200) or testing scgpt (which adds a new, untested variable on the RNA side)."
